spec_decode_attn: launch on Turing, whose 64 KB of shared memory the KV tile overflowed.

The split-KV verify attention picked its KV tile for sm86's 99 KB of shared memory per
block. At head size 256 a 64-token K/V tile needs 98,304 bytes (Triton's own
OutOfResources report, with the int8 per-token-head cache we ran), which Turing (sm75:
64 KB per block) cannot launch. So on a Quadro RTX 6000 or an RTX 20-series card the server died
at warm-up whenever VLLM_SPEC_DECODE_ATTN=1, which CTX=fast sets even with SPEC=off.

The launch now catches OutOfResources, which Triton raises when it loads the kernel and
before anything is queued, halves the KV tile and retries, and remembers the tile that
launched per (device, BLOCK_M, head size, cache dtype). Wherever the old tile launches,
which is every device it was written for, nothing changes and nothing is retried.

Measured on a Quadro RTX 6000 (sm75, 24 GB, 3.9 GB of it held by another process),
Qwen3.8-27B W4A16 (the default checkpoint after prepare/), --dtype float16, TRITON_ATTN,
int8_per_token_head KV, SPEC=off, MAX_LEN=20480. Without this patch the server dies at
warm-up with OutOfResources (98,304 bytes required, 65,536 available). With it, it starts
(55,680 KV tokens), answers correctly, and decodes 31.6 tok/s at one stream and 104.4
aggregate at four (~1.5k-token prompts).

